==English==

===Etymology===
Recorded since 1579 for "aromatic resin used for healing wounds and soothing pains", from {{etyl|la}} {{term|balsamum|lang=la}} (also the root of balm via Old French [[basme]], modern [[baume]]), itself from {{etyl|grc}} {{term|βάλσαμον||balsam|tr=balsamon|sc=polytonic|lang=grc}}," from Hebrew (''basam'') "spice", related to Arameic busma, Arabic (''basham'') "balsam, spice, perfume". There is an isolated Old English reference from c.1000. As a type of flowering plant of the [[Balsaminaceae]] or [[Impatiens]] family, it is attested from 1741.

===Pronunciation===
* {{enPR|bôlʹsəm}}, {{IPA|/ˈbɔːlsəm/}}, {{SAMPA|/"bO:ls@m/}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun|s|-}}

# A [[flower]]ing plant of the genus ''[[Impatiens]]''.
# A sweet-smelling [[oil]] or [[resin]] derived from various plants.
# A [[soothing]] [[ointment]].
# {{figurative}} Something soothing.
#: ''Classical music is a sweet '''balsam''' for our sorrows

